-- title:
--   Entropy of the word distribution
-- statement:
--   A self-similar system on the line consists of finitely many maps $f_i(x)=r_ix+b_i$ with $0<|r_i|<1$ and weights $w_i>0$ summing to $1$; a probability measure $\mu$ is self-similar when $\mu=\sum_iw_i(f_i)_*\mu$. The coding map sends an address $\omega\in\iota^{\mathbb N}$ to $\pi(\omega)=\lim_nf_{\omega_0}\circ\cdots\circ f_{\omega_{n-1}}(0)$, and the system has no exact overlaps when distinct finite words give distinct composed maps. Write $h_{\mathrm{sym}}=-\sum_iw_i\log w_i$ for the symbol entropy and $\chi_{\mathrm{nat}}=-\sum_iw_i\log|r_i|$ for the Lyapunov exponent, both with natural logarithms. For every $n$, the Shannon entropy of the distribution of words of length $n$, which gives $w$ the probability $\prod_jw_{w_j}$, is
--   $$
--   H\bigl((w_w)_{|w|=n}\bigr)=n\,h_{\mathrm{sym}} .
--   $$
--
--   Under no exact overlaps the word distribution is the distribution of composed maps, so the walk entropy grows exactly linearly.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MeasureTheory/SelfSimilar/NoOverlaps.lean#L150-L168

